WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.450
Choose two different numbers from one, two, three, four,

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.550
five and six.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.410
The order does not matter.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.050
How many choices have a sum of at least nine?

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.120
To avoid counting any pair twice, always name the

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.190
larger number first.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.270
Then organise the count by the larger number.

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:25.030
If the larger number is six, the smaller must be at

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:27.380
least three: three, four or five.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:28.580
That is three pairs.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.770
If the larger number is five, the smaller must be

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.060
four: one pair.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:37.140
If the larger is four, the best sum is four plus three,

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:38.560
which is only seven.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:39.500
No pairs.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.210
Three plus one is four.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.790
The pairs are three and six, four and six, five and six,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.210
and four and five.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.180
Check a different way, by the sum.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.580
Nine comes from three and six, or four and five.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.640
Ten comes from four and six.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.080
Eleven comes from five and six.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.560
Two plus one plus one is four.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.780
Notice that "at least nine" includes nine itself.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.300
Fix an order so nothing is counted twice,

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.200
sort into cases, then check another way.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:14.730
Rebuild this, then count the pairs from one to six whose

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.140
sum is at most five.
